# Vantigo application settings.
# Copy this file to `vantigo.env` and adjust the values.
# Every key here is read by apps/server/internal/config/config.go, which is
# the authoritative reference — field comments there document every default
# and every validation rule.

# --- Application and database ------------------------------------------------
# compose.yaml supplies DATABASE_URL directly (not through this file) so it
# can set a different role per service; do not set it here too. APP_URL is
# the public origin only (scheme + host [+ port]) — no path, no trailing
# slash. Accepted Host header values are derived from it plus loopback
# (localhost, 127.0.0.1, [::1]), so a request carrying any other Host is
# rejected with 400. Loopback stays permitted so the container health check,
# which reaches the app in-process on 127.0.0.1, is never rejected.
#
# The port below must match VANTIGO_PORT in `.env` (the host port compose.yaml
# publishes; both default to 8080). Nothing reconciles the two: host filtering
# compares hosts and ignores the port, so a mismatch raises no error at all —
# the stack still starts and still serves, but every invitation and
# password-reset link it mails points at a port nobody is listening on. Change
# one and you must change the other.
APP_URL=http://localhost:8080
# APP_BASE_PATH=/vantigo

# How long the process drains in-flight work on shutdown before it exits.
# compose.yaml's stop_grace_period on the vantigo service must exceed this,
# or Compose sends SIGKILL before the drain can finish.
# SHUTDOWN_TIMEOUT=30s

# --- Transport security ------------------------------------------------------
# READ THIS BEFORE EXPOSING THE STACK TO ANYONE.
#
# Transport is yours to choose and the application does not second-guess
# it. This quick-start stack serves http://localhost:8080 and talks to the
# bundled PostgreSQL container over the private compose network with no
# certificate authority anywhere. What that costs you the moment anything
# here is reachable from a network you do not control: session cookies and
# the bearer links in invitation and password-reset mails travel in the
# clear (an http APP_URL also means cookies are set without the Secure
# attribute), and so do the database credentials.
#
# For a real deployment:
#   - put the stack behind a TLS-terminating reverse proxy and set
#     APP_URL=https://vantigo.example.com;
#   - if PostgreSQL is reached across a network, add sslmode=verify-full
#     (or verify-ca) to DATABASE_URL on both services in compose.yaml —
#     see deploy/compose/README.md;
#   - keep SMTP_TLS at "starttls" or "implicit" (see below).
# See docs/src/content/docs/en/admin/transport-security.md.

# Emit the browser content security policy as report-only instead of
# enforcing it. Only needed while diagnosing a customized frontend; it
# disables the protection while set.
# CSP_REPORT_ONLY=1

# --- First-owner bootstrap ----------------------------------------------------
# By default no secret is needed: visit /setup on the fresh installation to
# create the first Owner account, which is also the SystemAdmin. Do so before
# anyone else can reach the address; setup closes once the Owner exists.
#
# Set BOOTSTRAP_OWNER_EMAIL to close /setup instead: while no Owner exists,
# every start mails that address an Owner invitation, once — a pending
# invitation is not re-sent before it expires, and an expired one is
# re-issued on the next start. The invitation follows the configured
# address: changing it while no Owner exists revokes the invitation already
# issued to the old address and mails the new one instead. An address some
# account already holds is never invited, only logged as a warning. A failed
# send is logged and the invitation is revoked; startup still succeeds, and
# the next start tries again. The account the invitation creates is the
# Owner and SystemAdmin, exactly like /setup's.
# BOOTSTRAP_OWNER_EMAIL=owner@example.com

# Process-wide key material (CSRF tokens, cookie signing, and encrypting
# TOTP secrets — the AES-256-GCM key is derived from this via HKDF-SHA256,
# one derived key per purpose). Required, at least 32 bytes. Generate with
# `openssl rand -base64 32` and never rotate it without a migration plan: a
# rotation invalidates every open session and every TOTP secret encrypted
# under the old value.
APP_SECRET=

# --- Identity and authentication ---------------------------------------------
# Outside development, the API requires privileged MFA (TOTP or a passkey)
# for the Owner and SystemAdmin roles by default, and an administrator whose
# session has not verified a second factor is refused by every module and
# control-plane endpoint until one is enrolled. Without MFA, a compromised
# Owner or SystemAdmin password alone is enough to reach every identity and
# tenant control-plane endpoint (https://github.com/vantigo-io/vantigo/issues/15).
# This quick-start stack has nobody around to enroll MFA right after /setup,
# so the requirement is turned off below. Both keys are needed:
# OWNERS_REQUIRE_MFA=0 turns it off, and OWNERS_ALLOW_INSECURE_NO_MFA=1 is
# the acknowledgement without which the API refuses to start with it off.
# Setting only the acknowledgement leaves the requirement on. For a real
# deployment, remove both lines, set OWNERS_REQUIRE_MFA=1, and enroll MFA for
# the Owner account from /settings/security right after /setup creates it
# (a session that has not enrolled yet is still permitted to reach the MFA
# setup endpoints, and the app holds it at that page, so this is never a
# lockout).
OWNERS_REQUIRE_MFA=0
OWNERS_ALLOW_INSECURE_NO_MFA=1
# OWNERS_REQUIRE_MFA=1
# MFA_ISSUER=Vantigo

# SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT=8h
# SESSION_PRIVILEGED_IDLE_TIMEOUT=2h
# SESSION_ABSOLUTE_LIFETIME=24h
# SESSION_PRIVILEGED_ABSOLUTE_LIFETIME=8h

# INVITATION_LIFETIME=168h
# INVITATION_ACCEPT_URL derives from APP_URL + APP_BASE_PATH by default and
# must contain the literal "{token}" placeholder.
# INVITATION_ACCEPT_URL=https://vantigo.example.com/invitations/accept?token={token}
# PASSWORD_RESET_URL derives from APP_URL + APP_BASE_PATH by default and must
# contain the literal "{email}" and "{token}" placeholders.
# PASSWORD_RESET_URL=https://vantigo.example.com/password-reset?email={email}&token={token}

# --- Single sign-on and SCIM ---------------------------------------------------
# Since migration 00058 the workforce OIDC provider and the SCIM token are set by
# an Owner on the Single sign-on card under Workspace administration and kept in
# identity.sso_settings; a change is in effect on every replica within 20 seconds,
# with no restart. The OIDC_* and SCIM_* variables below are read for one purpose
# only: the first API start that finds the row never written imports them into it
# (audited sso-settings.imported, with a warning naming them). Once the row is
# written they are ignored, and the next release refuses them at startup — remove
# them all together after the first start of this release. Nothing here is needed
# for a fresh installation: use the card. Workload identity (entra only) still
# needs the platform to project the token file; the card takes its path.
#
# OIDC_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_TOKEN_DIR is a live setting, not an imported one: the
# directory a token file saved on the card must lie in, so the deployment and
# not the Owner bounds what the server reads on the card's word. By default the
# directory of AZURE_FEDERATED_TOKEN_FILE (or of the import variable below),
# else the webhook's own.
# OIDC_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_TOKEN_DIR=/var/run/secrets/azure/tokens
# OIDC_PROVIDER=entra
# OIDC_AUTHORITY=https://login.microsoftonline.com/<tenant-guid>/v2.0
# OIDC_CLIENT_ID=<guid>
# OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=<inject-from-secret-store-without-trimming>
# OIDC_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_TOKEN_FILE=/var/run/secrets/azure/tokens/azure-identity-token
# OIDC_DISPLAY_NAME=Workforce SSO
# OIDC_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=example.com,example.org   (google only)
# SCIM_TOKEN=<inject-from-secret-store>
# SCIM_PREVIOUS_TOKEN=<inject-from-secret-store>
# SCIM_PREVIOUS_TOKEN_EXPIRES_AT=2026-01-01T00:00:00Z

# --- Application email (identity invitations and password recovery) -----------
# SMTP_HOST and SMTP_FROM below are REQUIRED FOR THE SERVER TO START AT ALL.
# They are not merely required in order to send mail: this stack runs outside
# development, where MAIL_DRIVER defaults to "smtp", and the smtp driver makes
# both of them mandatory. Configuration is loaded before the command runs, so
# leaving either blank fails the `migrate` job exactly as it fails `api` — and
# compose.yaml starts `vantigo` only once `vantigo-migrate` has completed
# successfully, so the whole stack stays down with:
#
#   invalid configuration:
#     SMTP_HOST: is required
#     SMTP_FROM: is required
#
# That is why they ship set rather than commented out: a fresh copy of this
# file boots as it stands. The placeholders are not a working relay, though.
# Until you replace them with one, the stack starts and serves normally but
# delivers no mail at all — which means nobody can be invited and no password
# can be recovered, since both of those flows are mail-delivered bearer links.
# Replace them before the deployment is used by anyone but you.
#
# SMTP_FROM is parsed as a plain address: "no-reply@example.com" is accepted,
# "Vantigo <no-reply@example.com>" is rejected at startup.
#
# MAIL_DRIVER is deliberately left unset, since it already defaults to "smtp"
# here. Do not set it to "log": that driver writes mail contents to the
# container log instead of sending them, and is rejected outside development
# because those mails carry bearer links.
# MAIL_DRIVER=smtp
SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
SMTP_FROM=no-reply@example.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USERNAME=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# "starttls" (default) or "implicit" negotiate TLS; "none" is plaintext — your
# choice, see docs/src/content/docs/en/admin/transport-security.md for what it costs.
# SMTP_TLS=starttls

# --- Business modules -----------------------------------------------------------
# Comma list of business modules this deployment enables. identity is always
# mounted and never listed here. Unset enables every module this binary can
# mount: customers, products, energy, communications, projects, time, expenses,
# invoices, staff, planning, calendar, entra (energy, communications, projects
# and invoices each require customers, time requires projects, planning
# requires both projects and staff, and entra requires staff; projects also uses products when it is enabled, and answers 409 on its
# billing-line endpoints when it is not; expenses requires nothing and reads
# projects only when it happens to be enabled; staff and calendar require
# nothing). To run a smaller install, list only what you want, for example:
# MODULES=customers,products
MODULES=customers,products,energy,communications,projects,time,expenses,invoices,staff,planning,calendar,entra

# Whether this container also runs every enabled module's background workers
# (outbox delivery, retention, attachment cleanup) in-process alongside
# serving. Default on, for this single-container deployment shape. A
# deployment that scales the API horizontally should set this to 0 on every
# API replica and run one dedicated `worker` container instead (see
# README.md "Background workers").
# WORKERS_IN_PROCESS=0

# --- Customers: Brønnøysundregisteret lookup ------------------------------------
# The customers module's only outbound HTTP dependency, used to look up
# Norwegian organizations. Defaults to the public production API.
# BRREG_BASE_URL=https://data.brreg.no
# BRREG_TIMEOUT=15s

# --- Customers: Peppol lookup -----------------------------------------------
# Whether a customer can receive an EHF (Peppol BIS Billing 3.0) invoice,
# asked of the Peppol network itself (POST .../peppol-lookup). Needs outbound
# DNS (UDP/TCP 53 to the configured or, when PEPPOL_DNS_SERVER is unset, the
# system resolver) to find a participant's SMP, and outbound HTTPS 443 to
# whichever SMP the Peppol SML zone's records name. Setting
# PEPPOL_LOOKUP_ENABLED=0 turns the feature off (the operation answers
# 503) without needing either.
# PEPPOL_LOOKUP_ENABLED=1
# The test network is participant.sml.test.tech.peppol.org.
# PEPPOL_SML_ZONE=participant.sml.prod.tech.peppol.org
# Unset uses the server's own name servers (/etc/resolv.conf); inside this
# Compose project that is Docker's embedded resolver (127.0.0.11, written to
# the container's /etc/resolv.conf), which forwards to the host as usual. A
# host with no /etc/resolv.conf at all — and no PEPPOL_DNS_SERVER set — makes
# every lookup answer 502 "no DNS server configured" rather than fail at
# startup.
# PEPPOL_DNS_SERVER=
# PEPPOL_TIMEOUT=10s

# --- Invoices: e-invoicing (EHF over Peppol) ------------------------------------
# Sending an issued document as EHF, through an access point (Storecove). Also
# needs PEPPOL_LOOKUP_ENABLED=1 (above) — a send re-checks the receiver on the
# Peppol network, so with the lookup off EHF is off too — an object store, and,
# in the Invoices settings, the access point's credentials and the seller's
# Peppol id. INVOICES_EHF_ENABLED=0 turns it off: a send answers 503
# ehf_unavailable and the invoices-ehf workers do not start. See
# docs/src/content/docs/en/admin/e-invoicing.md.
# INVOICES_EHF_ENABLED=1
# The Storecove API the access point calls: an absolute http or https URL, any
# trailing slash trimmed. The operator's setting, never the tenant's; point it
# at a mock host for a test. Storecove's sandbox is the same host with a
# sandbox key.
# INVOICES_STORECOVE_BASE_URL=https://api.storecove.com/api/v2/

# --- Invoices: Vipps MobilePay and the pay page ---------------------------------
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL is the https URL the pay page is reachable at from the
# internet; unset, there are no pay links. An origin plus APP_BASE_PATH and
# nothing else, and never APP_URL's host name on another port. Recommended: a
# host of its own, with the same base path — on it Vantigo serves only the pay
# page's allowlist and answers 404 to everything else. APP_URL's own origin
# publishes all of Vantigo. The gate keys on the Host header, and APP_URL's
# host and loopback stay admitted, so the proxy publishing the public host
# must send Host as the public host name, a constant (nginx:
# proxy_set_header Host pay.example.com; never $host) and drop every other
# name; anything else lets "Host: localhost" sent to the pay page's address
# reach all of Vantigo. It should also set TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS/
# TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS (below) and keep the public host's query strings — the
# pay link's token — out of its access and error logs. See
# docs/src/content/docs/en/admin/payments.md, "The public host".
# PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://pay.example.com
# Vipps MobilePay payments of invoices, on by default; an installation still
# needs the sales unit's credentials in the Invoices settings. 0 turns it off:
# every operation that would call Vipps answers 503 payments_unavailable.
# INVOICES_VIPPS_ENABLED=1
# The Vipps MobilePay API: an absolute http or https URL, any trailing slash
# trimmed. The operator's setting, never the tenant's; Vipps' test environment
# is https://apitest.vipps.no.
# INVOICES_VIPPS_BASE_URL=https://api.vipps.no

# --- Customers: registry workers ----------------------------------------------
# Two background workers keep a customer's registry data current without
# anybody clicking Refresh. They run wherever this deployment already runs
# workers: in `api` mode with WORKERS_IN_PROCESS=1, and in `worker` mode —
# never in `server` mode. Both elect one replica per cycle through a Postgres
# advisory lock, so running several is safe.
#
# The advisory locks are per DATABASE, and the two keys are fixed: two tenants
# pointing at the same database would take turns silently, one cycle at a time
# between them. Give each tenant its own database (what this directory's setup
# does) and they never meet — at the cost that N tenants poll Brreg
# independently, so the backfill below is N x ~100 entity reads an hour while
# they are catching up.
#
# The feed worker reads Brreg's incremental update feed (one small request per
# poll, matched against this installation's customers locally) and re-reads the
# entities it names, through the same path the Refresh button uses. It also
# sweeps: records whose last refresh failed, and Norwegian business customers
# that have no registry record at all yet (customers created before the record
# existed, 25 per cycle — about a hundred an hour — until they are caught up).
#
# TURNING THIS ON for the first time on an installation that already has
# customers is a one-off flood: every legacy Norwegian business customer gets
# its first registry record over the following day or two, and each one that the
# register disagrees with raises a dashboard attention item and a System
# timeline entry. There is no "dismiss" — the items stand until somebody fixes
# the legal name or archives the customer. Leave it at 0 until somebody can work
# through them.
#
# If the cursor ever stops moving while the sweep keeps working, Brreg has
# issued an update id its own API no longer accepts as a parameter (past int32):
# every feed request answers 400
# and an operator resets customers.registry_feed_cursor by hand (see
# docs/src/content/docs/en/reference/customers.md, "Registry workers").
# CUSTOMERS_REGISTRY_FEED_ENABLED=1
# CUSTOMERS_REGISTRY_FEED_POLL=15m
#
# The Peppol re-check worker asks the Peppol network again for customers whose
# stored answer has aged past CUSTOMERS_PEPPOL_RECHECK_AGE, and for customers
# already set to receive EHF invoices that have never been checked at all. It
# needs PEPPOL_LOOKUP_ENABLED=1 (above) — with the lookup off it never starts —
# and it never changes a customer's invoice delivery: a lapsed registration
# shows up as the billing profile's own warning, for a person to act on.
# CUSTOMERS_PEPPOL_RECHECK_ENABLED=1
# CUSTOMERS_PEPPOL_RECHECK_POLL=24h
# CUSTOMERS_PEPPOL_RECHECK_AGE=720h
#
# The anonymisation worker carries out the anonymisations people schedule for
# private-person customers (docs/src/content/docs/en/reference/customers.md, "Personal data and
# anonymisation"): once a day it takes every archived person whose day has come,
# at most fifty a cycle, and removes the person from the customer while keeping
# its number and the shape of its history. Turned off, scheduled dates wait.
# CUSTOMERS_ANONYMISATION_ENABLED=1
# CUSTOMERS_ANONYMISATION_POLL=24h
#
# The import worker checks and runs the customer files people upload
# (docs/src/content/docs/en/reference/customers.md, "Export, and import from a
# file"): it looks for a queued import every CUSTOMERS_IMPORT_POLL — and is
# nudged at once by the API when the workers run in-process — walks the file
# in chunks with progress, and sweeps imports older than
# CUSTOMERS_IMPORT_RETENTION. Turned off, an import queues and the page says it
# is waiting for the worker.
# CUSTOMERS_IMPORT_ENABLED=1
# CUSTOMERS_IMPORT_POLL=5s
# CUSTOMERS_IMPORT_RETENTION=720h

# --- Energy: the import worker ------------------------------------------------
# The energy import worker checks, runs and attaches the metering-point files
# people upload (docs/src/content/docs/en/reference/energy.md, "Importing
# metering points"): it looks for a queued import every ENERGY_IMPORT_POLL — and
# is nudged at once by the API when the workers run in-process — walks the file
# in chunks with progress, and sweeps imports older than ENERGY_IMPORT_RETENTION.
# Turned off, an import queues and the page says it is waiting for the worker.
# ENERGY_IMPORT_ENABLED=1
# ENERGY_IMPORT_POLL=5s
# ENERGY_IMPORT_RETENTION=720h

# --- Staff: the register's import and export ------------------------------------
# How many people one Staff import takes and one Staff export writes: one
# number for both, so a file the export wrote always imports back. At least
# 5000 (what the import template promises) and at most 20000: an import file is
# at most 5 MiB, and an export of more than about 20000 people would be larger.
# Every imported row is its own transaction, and a run at 5000 already takes
# most of the minute and a half a proxy in front of a hosted installation
# allows. See docs/src/content/docs/en/admin/staff.md, "Import and export the
# register".
# STAFF_IMPORT_MAX_ROWS=5000

# --- Staff: the retention worker ------------------------------------------------
# staff-retention erases what the law no longer lets the register keep: a
# person's personnel administration a grace period after their last employment
# ended, their health documents a longer period after it, and an employment's
# pay five years after the year it ended — both periods are Staff settings —
# and finishes deleting the documents' objects from the object store, retrying
# a refusal until the store confirms. Turned off, nothing is erased on a
# schedule (POST /retention/run still runs it by hand). The poll is how often
# it looks: at least 1m; an erase is idempotent, so hourly keeps "once a day".
# See docs/src/content/docs/en/admin/staff.md, "Retention".
# STAFF_RETENTION_ENABLED=1
# STAFF_WORKERS_POLL=1h

# --- Staff: the accounts worker -------------------------------------------------
# staff-accounts gives the people whose account flag HR turned on a user in
# identity: prepared, disabled, createBeforeDays before the start, enabled on
# the day with an invitation to choose a password, disabled the day after the
# last employment ends, enabled again on a rehire. With a flag on it creates
# that person's pending, disabled user even before an Owner names the
# employee roles, and enables nobody until they are named. Turned off,
# nothing is done on a schedule —
# a leaver keeps their access until it is on again or an Owner disables them.
# It shares STAFF_WORKERS_POLL above; a failed step is retried at the next poll
# three times, then daily — a leaver's deactivation at every poll until it
# succeeds. The process running it must carry the same OIDC_*
# and SCIM_* settings as the API process: where they differ it disables
# leavers and holds every other step (mode_mismatch).
# See docs/src/content/docs/en/admin/staff.md, "The accounts worker".
# STAFF_ACCOUNTS_ENABLED=1

# --- Staff: the competence worker -----------------------------------------------
# staff-competence mails a person and their managers once when a certificate
# the person holds comes within its competence's reminder lead days of
# expiring, and once more when it has expired; each row is mailed once for
# each, and a renewal starts afresh. Turned off, nobody is mailed — the
# dashboard still shows expiring and expired certificates. It shares
# STAFF_WORKERS_POLL above.
# See docs/src/content/docs/en/admin/staff.md.
# STAFF_COMPETENCE_ENABLED=1

# --- Calendar: the workers ----------------------------------------------------
# calendar-cancellations erases the appointments that were cancelled — deleted
# after someone outside the company had been sent an invitation — once their
# last day has passed: a single appointment's end, a bounded series' last
# occurrence, and for a series that never ends ninety days after it was
# cancelled. At most 200 a cycle. Turned off, cancelled appointments are kept
# until it is on again. The poll is how often it looks: at least 1m.
# CALENDAR_CANCELLATIONS_ENABLED=1
# CALENDAR_WORKERS_POLL=1h
#
# calendar-subscriptions fetches the calendars people subscribe to from
# elsewhere, through the outbound guard (no private address, no redirect, at
# most 1 MiB). It wakes every five minutes and fetches each subscription once a
# poll, or less often when the feed asks for it, plus a little jitter. Turned
# off, every subscription shows its last read until it is on again; saving one
# or Refresh now still fetches it once. The poll: at least 5m.
# CALENDAR_SUBSCRIPTIONS_ENABLED=1
# CALENDAR_SUBSCRIPTIONS_POLL=30m
#
# calendar-mailbox-sync keeps the copies people sync to Outlook (Calendar ->
# Settings -> Outlook, once an Owner has turned on Calendar sync in the Entra
# module). It wakes every minute; an appointment change makes its people due at
# once, and everyone else is synced once a poll. Turned off, nothing is written
# to or removed from anyone's mailbox until it is on again. The poll: at least 1m.
# CALENDAR_MAILBOX_SYNC_ENABLED=1
# CALENDAR_MAILBOX_SYNC_POLL=15m
#
# See docs/src/content/docs/en/admin/calendar.md.

# --- Object storage ---------------------------------------------------------
# Unset (the default) leaves storage unconfigured: the process still starts,
# but every storage operation (Communications attachments, Expenses receipts,
# Invoices issuing and PDF downloads) fails with a clear "not configured"
# error instead of the process refusing to boot — a receipt upload or
# download answers 503, logged on the server side. Expenses needs this
# configured the moment anybody tries to attach a receipt, whether or not the
# receipt rule (receiptRequiredOver) requires one; see docs/src/content/docs/en/reference/expenses.md.
# Invoices refuses every issue with 503 storage_unavailable until it is set,
# because an issued number whose PDF could never be stored may not exist;
# see docs/src/content/docs/en/reference/invoices.md.
# "fs" is the only supported provider today.
# STORAGE_PROVIDER=fs
# STORAGE_FS_ROOT=/data/vantigo-storage
# Relaxes the fs driver's group/world-writable root check. Only accepted
# when APP_ENV=development; rejected at startup outside it.
# STORAGE_FS_ALLOW_INSECURE_ROOT=1

# --- Communications: attachments and retention ----------------------------------
# COMMUNICATIONS_ATTACHMENT_MAX_BYTES=10485760
# How long a staged attachment stays valid before the retention worker
# sweeps it.
# COMMUNICATIONS_UPLOAD_EXPIRY=24h
# How old terminal communication history must be before the retention
# worker deletes it.
# COMMUNICATIONS_RETENTION_DAYS=365
# COMMUNICATIONS_RETENTION_BATCH_SIZE=100
# How often the retention worker runs a cycle.
# COMMUNICATIONS_RETENTION_POLL=1h
#
# Per-channel SMTP credentials (the mailboxes messages actually send through)
# are configured through the Communications API and sealed at rest with a key
# derived from APP_SECRET, not set as environment variables here.

# --- Communications: AI draft and suggestion features ----------------------------
# Both operations answer 503 ai_unavailable unless all three of these are
# set and the provider is "openai". Off by default.
# COMMUNICATIONS_AI_ENABLED=1
# COMMUNICATIONS_AI_PROVIDER=openai
# COMMUNICATIONS_AI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini
# COMMUNICATIONS_AI_API_KEY=<inject-from-secret-store>

# --- Reverse proxy ------------------------------------------------------------
# When running behind a TLS-terminating reverse proxy, trust exactly that
# proxy so forwarded scheme/host/client-address headers are honored.
# TRUSTED_PROXY_HOPS=1
# TRUSTED_PROXY_CIDRS=10.0.0.0/24

# --- Management listener --------------------------------------------------------
# A second, private HTTP listener a control plane polls for the running
# version, the bootstrap state and usage: GET /management/status with
# "Authorization: Bearer <MANAGEMENT_TOKEN>". Unset (the default) disables it;
# the two variables must be set together. It runs in the api and server
# commands, never in worker. It has no host filter, so that it can be reached
# by service name — do NOT publish or route this port. The token must be at
# least 32 characters with no whitespace. See docs/src/content/docs/en/admin/management-listener.md.
# MANAGEMENT_PORT=9090
# MANAGEMENT_TOKEN=<inject-from-secret-store>

# --- Logging -------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOG_LEVEL=info

# --- Branding ------------------------------------------------------------------
# APP_TITLE=Vantigo
# APP_LOGO_URL=/logo.svg
# APP_SUPPORT_EMAIL=support@example.com
# APP_SUPPORT_PHONE=+47 00 00 00 00
# APP_SUPPORT_URL=https://example.com/support

# --- Observability ------------------------------------------------------------
# OpenTelemetry traces, metrics and logs. Off unless an OTLP endpoint is set;
# these are the standard OTel SDK environment variables, read directly by the
# OTLP exporter rather than by config.go.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-collector:4317
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL=grpc
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=x-api-key=secret
